| g5-group-inquiry; g5-share-real-audience: a widened group question, a product for a real audience | 4 | g6-team-product; g6-make-and-improve | A team's sharing plan and a new idea; a made product improved by trial |

Every grade 5 row hands on to a grade 6 row, and each grade 6 row's assumes names it back. Grades 6 to 8 are one
course taught about one session every two weeks; its grade 6 year picks these up.

## Models carried

| Model | Where it comes from | Introduced here | Used again here |
|---|---|---|---|
| research-cycle | Library kindergarten (k-class-inquiry), through grade 4 (g4-narrow-and-plan, model_out); here g5-share-real-audience (model_in and model_out) | Not new: unit 1 opens with the six-step cycle card, held for one day | Every unit runs the whole cycle once on its host question; unit 4 runs it as a group to a real audience |
| search-plan | Library grade 3 (g3-key-words), through grade 4 (g4-search-more-kinds, model_out); here g5-search-revise (model_in and model_out) and g5-search-log-look-back (model_in) | Not new: unit 1 adds the query change and the log | Units 2 to 4 keep a search log on their inquiry days, as review |
| citation | Library grade 2 (g2-credit-line), through grade 4 (g4-honest-use, model_out); here g5-bibliography (model_in and model_out) | Not new: unit 3 grows the source list into a bibliography to a model | Unit 4's product ends with its bibliography, as review |
| lateral-reading | New in grade 5 (g5-first-lateral-check, model_out): leave the page, ask who is behind it and why it was made | Unit 2, show-me day 2 | Unit 3's source sets and unit 4's group sources are checked laterally where a page is unfamiliar, as review |

The models go on to grades 6 to 8 (g6-keep-questioning, g6-search-strategy, g6-quote-paraphrase, g6-lateral-reading and
g6-group-wiki, model_in).

## Units

| # | Unit and compelling question | Days | Skills | Owned standards | Inquiry phases | What students learn | Why this unit, here | Check set adds | Sensitive topics and teacher notes |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | Research or guess. *Which questions can research answer, and what do we do when a search is not working?* | 7 | g5-research-or-guess; g5-search-revise; g5-search-log-look-back | AASL.IV.A.1; AASL.I.D.4 | Wonder, investigate (search), reflect | Judging which questions facts can answer: sorting questions into researchable, opinion, and needs data no one has yet, and rewriting an opinion question as one research can answer ("Which is best?" becomes "Which uses less water?") (AASL.IV.A.1, owned here: "Decide when a question needs research rather than a guess," checked alone). Starting from a list of key words and synonyms, then on an invented results page changing a query when results are poor (narrower, broader, a synonym, an exact phrase), reading the source name under each result before choosing it, and skipping results marked as ads. Keeping a search log during an inquiry (terms tried, what came back, what they did next) and at the end looking back at it to decide what to do differently next time (AASL.I.D.4, owned here: "Look back on how a search went to choose what to do next," checked alone). Revisited: turning a topic into questions (AASL.I.A.1, owned by g3-research-questions, grade 3), with a tentative answer on the plan line; searching more than one kind of source (AASL.IV.B.1, owned by g4-search-more-kinds, grade 4). Out: planning a search before typing and choosing among results by snippet (grade 6, g6-search-strategy); search operators and filters (grades 9 to 12). Opening retrieval: day 2, narrow one topic and write a plan line (g4-narrow-and-plan); day 3, one question searched on two printed kinds (g4-search-more-kinds), and, inside the sort, which of two own questions research can answer (g4-own-question). Days: show-me (hook; researchable, opinion, needs data; rewriting an opinion question), show-me (the key-word list, changing a query, the source name, the ad; the log), practice (sort six questions; fix three poor searches on invented results pages, logging each), inquiry (judge the host question, list key words, search and log), inquiry (look back at the log, write the next-step line, find the answer, a pair share and the self-check), end-of-unit assessment, respond. Skill card: "research, opinion or needs data; words, try, read the source, skip the ad, log, look back". End-of-unit evidence (written sitting, about 26 minutes for 30): six new questions sorted into the three kinds and one opinion question rewritten (items and rubric, about 8 minutes); on a new invented results page, a poor query changed twice with the reason for each, the source names read and the ad marked (rubric, about 10 minutes); a new sample search log, with the next-step line that follows from what happened (rubric, about 8 minutes). Tools: the bat source set, the question sort cards, three invented results pages, the search log sheet (Curio Class writes all). Host: the "what is everything made of?" questions of science grade 5 unit 1 (days 1 to 17), sorted as researchable or needing an investigation; stand-alone: "How do bats find their way in the dark?" | First because every later unit starts from a researchable question and a search that may need changing; the search log begun here is kept on every later inquiry day | Candidate: the continuum's grade 5 cells on recognizing gaps in what one knows and a manageable topic, at the judging step; its tentative-answer cell as the plan line | Neutral topic (bats; matter). The rewritten opinion questions stay on neutral things (which material, which plant), never on people or groups. The invented results pages show no real site and an ad for an invented product. The stop rule stays on any device. Whose voices: the unit records the makers of its sources (C12) |
| 2 | Choosing and checking sources. *Which sources earn a place in our research, and who is behind them?* | 6 | g5-choose-sources; g5-first-lateral-check | AASL.IV.A.3; AASL.IV.B.3 | Investigate (sources), construct, reflect | Weighing print, video and online sources for relevance, date and reading level, choosing three for a question and writing one line on why each earns its place; one choice may be a group-written online encyclopedia used as a starting map, checked through its references (AASL.IV.A.3, owned here: "Choose which sources to use, and say why each one earns its place," checked alone). Before reading an unfamiliar page closely, leaving it: searching the maker's name in a new tab or on a printed "what others say" card, and deciding who is behind the page and why it was made (AASL.IV.B.3, owned here: "Question each source's maker and purpose before trusting what it says," checked alone); the lateral reading model introduced here, the first time library teaches leaving the page as a method. Begun here, owned later: judging sites many people write and edit together (AASL.IV.C.1, owned by g6-group-wiki, grade 6). Revisited: using information and devices responsibly (AASL.VI.A.1, owned by g3-safe-searching, grade 3), the stop rule. Out: going upstream to the original of a statistic or quote, and judging a claim's fairness (grade 6, g6-lateral-reading); edit history (grade 6). Opening retrieval (day 2): sort three invented pages by purpose and find the ad (g4-primary-secondary). Days: show-me (hook; weighing six sources for one question, the reason line, the group-written encyclopedia as a map), show-me (leaving the page: the maker's name searched or looked up on the card; who is behind it and why), practice (choose three of six sources with reasons; lateral checks on three invented pages), inquiry (choose three sources for the host question, laterally check one unfamiliar page, a pair share and the self-check), end-of-unit assessment, respond. Skill card: "fits my question, current, I can read it, why it earns its place; leave the page, who is behind it, why made". End-of-unit evidence (written sitting, about 25 minutes for 30): from a new set of six (print, a described video, online pages), three chosen with a reason line each (rubric, about 12 minutes); two new invented pages, each with its "what others say" card: who is behind the page and why it was made, decided before reading it closely, with the line that shows it (rubric, about 13 minutes). Tools: the sea turtle six-source set, invented pages and their cards, an invented copy of a group-written encyclopedia article with references (Curio Class writes all). Host: the rainforest research of ELA grade 5 unit 2 (days 42 to 80) (e5-locate-sources), in the same weeks; stand-alone: "How do sea turtles find their way back to the beach where they hatched?" | After unit 1 because a search has now returned results, and the student must choose among them and check the unfamiliar ones; before unit 3, whose notes come from sources chosen and checked this way; social studies grade 5 has already taught sourcing on its own documents | Candidate: the continuum's grade 5 cells on evaluating information within a source for accuracy and comprehensiveness, as the reason line's third test; its verify-the-truth cells as the lateral check's last line | Invented pages only on practice and check days, and the stop rule on any device (the row's note). Social studies grade 5 unit 3 teaches sourcing and corroboration on revolution documents; library borrows only the move and runs on sea turtle pages (K to 5 neutral rule). No page names a real site or organization. Whose voices: the unit records the makers of its sources (C12) |
| 3 | Many sources, one answer. *Where do our sources agree about redwood trees, where do they differ, and how do we credit every one?* | 6 | g5-evidence-notes; g5-bibliography | None | Investigate (evidence across sources), construct, express, reflect | Taking answer-only notes from three sources on one question, marking where the sources agree and where they differ on a fact (a measurement, a date, a count), and arranging the notes in a simple outline or mind map with help. Building a bibliography to a model for every source in a product (author, title, date, publisher or site, and the kind of source), print, video and web alike, and explaining why an unnamed page is a weak source. Revisited: evidence that answers, not related facts (AASL.I.B.1) and notes sorted by a plan (AASL.IV.B.4), both owned by g4-evidence-notes, grade 4; naming who made each work (AASL.VI.B.2, owned by g2-credit-line, grade 2); explaining honest use (AASL.VI.A.2, owned by g4-honest-use, grade 4). Begun here, owned later: comparing how several sources with different views treat a question (AASL.II.B.2, owned by g7-sources-disagree, grade 7); here only facts differ. Out: one set citation style (grade 7); note cards keeping paraphrase and quotation apart (grade 6). Opening retrieval: day 2, two answer-only notes from a short paragraph (g4-evidence-notes); day 3, fix one not-yet-honest case (g4-honest-use). Days: show-me (hook; notes from three sources, the agree and differ marks, the outline), show-me (the bibliography model; print, video and web entries; the unnamed page), practice (three short passages marked for agreement; five sources into a bibliography; two entries fixed), inquiry (notes from three sources for the host question, the marks, an outline, a bibliography, a pair share and the self-check), end-of-unit assessment, respond. Skill card: "answer only, three sources, agree or differ, outline; who, what, when, where from, what kind". End-of-unit evidence (written sitting, about 25 minutes for 30): three new short passages on one question: answer-only notes with each agreement and difference marked (rubric, about 13 minutes); a bibliography for four new sources (a book, a described video, two web pages, one with no maker) in the model's order, and one line on why the unnamed page is weak (rubric and item, about 12 minutes). Tools: the redwood three-source set with its differing heights and ages, the bibliography model card (Curio Class writes all; any measurement is invented for practice or carries its free source and an as-of date). Host: the rainforest research of ELA grade 5 unit 2 (days 42 to 80) (e5-research), or the water distribution question of science grade 5 unit 5 (days 85 to 99); stand-alone: "How tall do redwood trees grow, and how old can they get?" | After unit 2 because the student now holds three chosen, checked sources and must use them together; the bibliography follows the notes it credits; before unit 4, whose group product needs pooled notes and a full bibliography | Candidate: the continuum's grade 5 cells on notetaking chosen for the purpose and on organizing notes into an outline or mind map, at the notes step; its credit-with-page-number cell, as one optional field of the model | Differences are factual only (K to 5 neutral rule; the row's note), never views on politics, religion, identity groups or conflict. Honest use without shaming (APPROACH.md, Sensitive ground, item 4): a copied sentence is fixed with the student privately. Whose voices: the unit records the makers of its sources (C12) |
| 4 | Researching together for a real audience. *What can our group find out about how wetlands clean water, and who needs to hear it?* | 6 | g5-group-inquiry; g5-share-real-audience | AASL.III.A.1; AASL.I.C.4 | Wonder, investigate (with others), construct, express, reflect | A group widens a question by researching together: members split it into subquestions, each brings back findings, and the group pools them into a bigger answer than any one member had, saying how the question grew (AASL.III.A.1, owned here: "Look for chances to widen and deepen a question by researching with others," checked alone: each student explains what researching with others added). The group revises its research product and shares it with real people who have a reason to care (a younger class, families at a display, the school office), picks the form for that audience and answers the audience's questions (AASL.I.C.4, owned here: "Share finished research with real people who have a reason to care," checked alone). Revisited: working out a new way to find out (AASL.V.C.2, owned by g4-class-survey, grade 4), where a subquestion needs a class survey; making a product from research (AASL.I.B.3, owned by g3-make-product, grade 3); using advice to revise (AASL.I.C.3, owned by g4-revise-from-advice, grade 4); the year's search log, lateral check, notes and bibliography, as review. Out: a team's choice of sharing tools and an idea no member had alone (grade 6, g6-team-product). Opening retrieval (day 2): say which subquestion a survey could answer and rewrite one closed question as open (g4-class-survey, g4-asking-others); inside the revision day, one log line for a piece of advice (g4-revise-from-advice). Days: show-me (hook; splitting a question into subquestions, pooling, saying how it grew; choosing a real audience), inquiry (members research their subquestions with the year's moves and bring back findings), inquiry (pool the findings; choose the form for the audience; draft the product and its bibliography), revision (teacher and peer advice, the change log as review, the audience's likely questions), share with the real audience (about 20 minutes, then the self-check), end-of-unit assessment. Skill card: "split, research, bring back, pool, how did it grow; who cares, what form, answer their questions". End-of-unit evidence (about 27 minutes for 30): the shared product is scored on its rubric on the share day (the form fits the audience, the findings are clear, the audience's questions answered from the research), with the draft day, the written advice and the revision counted in the unit's days above; the written sitting, all at once, asks each student to explain what researching with the group added to the question and how it grew (rubric, about 12 minutes), and to choose and justify a form for a new audience card (rubric, about 8 minutes). Tools: the wetland group source set, the subquestion and pooling sheet, display paper, audience cards (Curio Class writes all). Host: protecting water by design, science grade 5 unit 6 (days 100 to 117) (s5-protect-resources), in the same weeks; stand-alone: "How do wetlands clean water, and who in our school should know?" | Last because it gathers the year: a researchable question and a logged search (unit 1), chosen and checked sources (unit 2) and notes with a bibliography (unit 3) are what each member brings; the real audience needs a finished, revised product | Candidate: the continuum's grade 5 cells on working with others to research and come to new understandings, and on soliciting and listening to others' ideas, at the pooling; presenting so evidence reaches the audience, at the share | The host keeps to how water is kept clean, never a local dispute or who is to blame (K to 5 neutral rule). The audience is inside school (a younger class, families at a school display, the school office); audience questions are answered from the research, and no student is asked about home. Groups are mixed by the teacher, never by background. Whose voices: the unit records the makers of its sources (C12) |

## Course-level checks

| Check | After unit | Check days | Respond days | What it checks |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1, mid-year | 2 | 1 | 1 | Week 14, one session, a written sitting, about 25 minutes for 30. It samples unit 1 on new cases: five questions sorted as researchable, opinion or needs data, one rewritten; a poor query on a new invented results page changed twice with reasons and the ad marked; a next-step line for a sample search log. Unit 2, which ended the week before, is not re-tested: its end-of-unit result is reused unscored, as a class count of the students who met each rubric step, written on this check's sheet. The respond day (week 15) reteaches the missed step in small groups from the sheet, ending with one fresh try |
| 2, year-end | 4 | 1 | 2 | Week 28, one session, a written sitting, about 27 minutes for 30. It samples units 1 to 3 on new cases: one question judged and a query changed (unit 1); three sources chosen from five with reasons and one invented page checked laterally on its card (unit 2); notes from two short passages with an agreement and a difference marked, and a bibliography for three sources (unit 3). Unit 4, which ended in week 27, is reused unscored as a class count on the sheet, as at mid-year. Respond day 1 (week 29) reteaches the gaps in small groups from the sheet. Respond day 2 (week 30) is the year's reflect step: each student looks back over the year's search logs and writes what they would do differently on the next search and one question they still want to chase, which grade 6 picks up (g6-keep-questioning) |

Placement: neither check falls in a week where docs/courses/CALENDAR.md shows a grade 5 core check (science 10, 21
