171: ## Units
172: 
173: | # | Unit and compelling question | Days | Skills | Owned standards | Inquiry phases | What students learn | Why this unit, here | Check set adds | Sensitive topics and teacher notes |
174: |---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
175: | 1 | Research or guess. *Which questions can research answer, and what do we do when a search is not working?* | 7 | g5-research-or-guess; g5-search-revise; g5-search-log-look-back | AASL.IV.A.1; AASL.I.D.4 | Wonder, investigate (search), reflect | Judging which questions facts can answer: sorting questions into researchable, opinion, and needs data no one has yet, and rewriting an opinion question as one research can answer ("Which is best?" becomes "Which uses less water?") (AASL.IV.A.1, owned here: "Decide when a question needs research rather than a guess," checked alone). Starting from a list of key words and synonyms, then on an invented results page changing a query when results are poor (narrower, broader, a synonym, an exact phrase), reading the source name under each result before choosing it, and skipping results marked as ads. Keeping a search log during an inquiry (terms tried, what came back, what they did next) and at the end looking back at it to decide what to do differently next time (AASL.I.D.4, owned here: "Look back on how a search went to choose what to do next," checked alone). Revisited: turning a topic into questions (AASL.I.A.1, owned by g3-research-questions, grade 3), with a tentative answer on the plan line; searching more than one kind of source (AASL.IV.B.1, owned by g4-search-more-kinds, grade 4). Out: planning a search before typing and choosing among results by snippet (grade 6, g6-search-strategy); search operators and filters (grades 9 to 12). Opening retrieval: day 2, narrow one topic and write a plan line (g4-narrow-and-plan); day 3, one question searched on two printed kinds (g4-search-more-kinds), and, inside the sort, which of two own questions research can answer (g4-own-question). Days: show-me (hook; researchable, opinion, needs data; rewriting an opinion question), show-me (the key-word list, changing a query, the source name, the ad; the log), practice (sort six questions; fix three poor searches on invented results pages, logging each), inquiry (judge the host question, list key words, search and log), inquiry (look back at the log, write the next-step line, find the answer, a pair share and the self-check), end-of-unit assessment, respond. Skill card: "research, opinion or needs data; words, try, read the source, skip the ad, log, look back". End-of-unit evidence (written sitting, about 26 minutes for 30): six new questions sorted into the three kinds and one opinion question rewritten (items and rubric, about 8 minutes); on a new invented results page, a poor query changed twice with the reason for each, the source names read and the ad marked (rubric, about 10 minutes); a new sample search log, with the next-step line that follows from what happened (rubric, about 8 minutes). Tools: the bat source set, the question sort cards, three invented results pages, the search log sheet (Curio Class writes all). Host: the "what is everything made of?" questions of science grade 5 unit 1 (days 1 to 17), sorted as researchable or needing an investigation; stand-alone: "How do bats find their way in the dark?" | First because every later unit starts from a researchable question and a search that may need changing; the search log begun here is kept on every later inquiry day | Candidate: the continuum's grade 5 cells on recognizing gaps in what one knows and a manageable topic, at the judging step; its tentative-answer cell as the plan line | Neutral topic (bats; matter). The rewritten opinion questions stay on neutral things (which material, which plant), never on people or groups. The invented results pages show no real site and an ad for an invented product. The stop rule stays on any device. Whose voices: the unit records the makers of its sources (C12) |
