rd: "fits my question, current, I can read it, why it earns its place; leave the page, who is behind it, why made". End-of-unit evidence (written sitting, about 25 minutes for 30): from a new set of six (print, a described video, online pages), three chosen with a reason line each (rubric, about 12 minutes); two new invented pages, each with its "what others say" card: who is behind the page and why it was made, decided before reading it closely, with the line that shows it (rubric, about 13 minutes). Tools: the sea turtle six-source set, invented pages and their cards, an invented copy of a group-written encyclopedia article with references (Curio Class writes all). Host: the rainforest research of ELA grade 5 unit 2 (days 42 to 80) (e5-locate-sources), in the same weeks; stand-alone: "How do sea turtles find their way back to the beach where they hatched?" | After unit 1 because a search has now returned results, and the student must choose among them and check the unfamiliar ones; before unit 3, whose notes come from sources chosen and checked this way; social studies grade 5 has already taught sourcing on its own documents | Candidate: the continuum's grade 5 cells on evaluating information within a source for accuracy and comprehensiveness, as the reason line's third test; its verify-the-truth cells as the lateral check's last line | Invented pages only on practice and check days, and the stop rule on any device (the row's note). Social studies grade 5 unit 3 teaches sourcing and corroboration on revolution documents; library borrows only the move and runs on sea turtle pages (K to 5 neutral rule). No page names a real site or organization. Whose voices: the unit records the makers of its sources (C12) |
