## Findings, most serious first

### 1. Unit 1 does not complete its promised inquiry cycle

**Severity:** Moderate  
**LAYER:** frame  
**REPEAT:** yes, grade 3 independent frame read, finding 2; kindergarten pilot�?Ts inquiry repair  
**Confidence:** firm

**Location:** Frame, Inquiry arc bullet, lines 55 to 61; Unit 1, line 174.

**What is wrong:** Library APPROACH requires a complete inquiry cycle and a shared product in every unit. Unit 1�?Ts inquiry day specifies a plan, searches, a partner reading the plan and a self-check. It never explicitly requires students to read a located source, judge its usefulness, use information to answer their question or share that answer. Its assessment likewise requests plans without explicitly requiring a record of carrying them out.

**Why it matters:** A writer can follow these instructions and produce a planning exercise without the research outcome that makes the planning useful. The [AASL learner framework](https://standards.aasl.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/07/250918-AASL-framework-for-learners.pdf) includes implementation within I.B.2.

**Exact fix:**

- Replace the inquiry-day description with:

  > Plan and search for one narrowed host question. Locate short answering passages through two printed source kinds, retrieve the earlier maker-and-purpose check, and record what each search actually found. Write a brief answer with its source credit, share it with a partner, then complete the self-check.

- Add the answering passages and an answer-and-credit box to the materials.
- In the assessment, require the student to execute the search plan on the supplied materials and record a located result from each kind. Score both the fit of the plan and evidence of its implementation.
- Update the Inquiry phases cell to include construct and express.

Use earlier-grade answering and credit routines as review. Keep independent category-making and formal lateral reading in their existing places.

### 2. Unit 3�?Ts assessment substitutes written questions and dictation for interaction

**Severity:** Moderate  
**LAYER:** frame  
**REPEAT:** yes, the incomplete individual-performance evidence pattern in the library progression review and grade 3 independent read  
**Confidence:** firm

**Location:** Unit 3, line 176, end-of-unit evidence.

**What is wrong:** Students write open questions and record an answer the teacher reads aloud. This checks question construction and recording, but does not show each student asking someone a question, attending to that person�?Ts perspective or responding thoughtfully. Those actions are part of the taught row and the intended II.D.2 outcome. The [AASL framework](https://standards.aasl.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/07/250918-AASL-framework-for-learners.pdf) distinguishes interest in others�?T perspectives from merely recording information.

**Why it matters:** A student could meet the written rubric without demonstrating the interpersonal skill this unit owns.

**Exact fix:** Replace the dictated-answer component with a brief assessed exchange on a new, teacher-approved neutral topic:

> Each student asks an open question, accurately restates the partner�?Ts answer and asks one relevant follow-up. The teacher records these three outcomes separately for each student. The written record supports the observation.

Retain the written explanation of the group�?Ts investigation plan and the three planned questions. Budget approximately eight minutes for written work and eighteen minutes for observed exchanges, about seventy seconds per pair for fifteen pairs. Partners complete the written work while awaiting their observed exchange. Validate that allocation when the assessment materials are built.

