Anchors: an original Curio Class novel read whole (a present-day girl whose family loses its home after her father's death and starts over doing farm work in another state; chapters named for the crops they harvest; about 30,000 to 40,000 words, measure target about 750 to 950); articles of the Universal Declaration of Human Rights in the official 1948 text, each with Curio Class's own short plain explainer (measure target about 850 to 1010); a short original two-scene play from the novel's world, the model for the monologue, and a fresh short scene read alone at the end of the unit. Quoting accurately when explaining and inferring, in talk and in writing (RL.5.1, RI.5.1, W.5.9a on the novel and play, W.5.9b on the declaration articles); a theme from how the girl responds to each challenge, and a summary (RL.5.2); two characters' responses to one event compared (RL.5.3); metaphors and similes interpreted in a passage, idioms and proverbs, synonyms, antonyms and homographs (RL.5.4, L.5.5a to c); how the chapters fit the novel's arc and the scenes fit the play (RL.5.5); stories, the play and poems read in the band, the fresh scene read alone (RL.5.10); two or more main ideas of a declaration article and an explainer, with a summary, and a summary of a short talk that shows a simple chart (numbers within grade 4 math), told from the words and the chart together (RI.5.2, SL.5.2); the unit's words, with contrast and addition words (however, although, moreover) (RI.5.4, L.5.6), context of cause and comparison, Greek and Latin roots, references for pronunciation and meaning (L.5.4a to c). Writing: daily exit tickets quoting the text (W.5.10) and a literary essay on how a character's response to a challenge shows a theme, connected to one declaration article: a general observation and focus, logical grouping, quotations, linking words (in contrast, especially), precise words, a conclusion (W.5.2a to e, W.5.4); a monologue in the voice of a character of the novel, oriented, with description, pacing, sensory detail and a conclusion (W.5.3a to e), built from short notes on the novel's setting and the farm work (W.5.8 previewed in its grade 4 form; taught in unit 2), planned, critiqued, rewritten where a new approach works better and edited (W.5.5), then performed and recorded as audio on a classroom device; the literary essay's revision typed (W.5.6 begins; two pages in a sitting and the shared-document exchange are continued in unit 2). Talk: discussions prepared, with roles, elaborating on others and drawing conclusions (SL.5.1a to d). Grammar from the novel's sentences: conjunctions, prepositions, interjections (L.5.1a), perfect tenses (L.5.1b), tense for time and sequence (L.5.1c), tense shifts (L.5.1d), correlative conjunctions (L.5.1e), expanding, combining and reducing sentences (L.5.3a; 3b, varieties of English, is taught in unit 2 with the narrators). Conventions in editing: series commas, introductory commas, yes and no, tag questions ("It's hard, isn't it?"), direct address, titles, spelling (L.5.2a to e). Skills block from day 1: the long-word screen, then the sequence (RF.5.3a); fluency on the novel's passages and the monologue rehearsed for its recording (RF.5.4a to c). Retrieval: grade 4 fragments and run-ons and discussion roles; the fall oral reading check is a diagnostic. The unit's word list. Lettered parts of these standards taught in another unit: L.5.3b (unit 2, unit 4).
2 | The rainforest. "How do the living things of El Yunque depend on each other, and how do people protect it?" | 39 | e5-relationships; e5-compare-structures; e5-locate-sources; e5-research; e5-narrator-influence; e5-genre-compare | RI.5.3; RI.5.5; RI.5.7; W.5.7; W.5.8; RL.5.6; L.5.3b; RL.5.9 | Not used (ELA). Leans on science grade 5 (matter and energy in ecosystems)
Anchors: an original Curio Class account of a canopy scientist's year in El Yunque National Forest in Puerto Rico (measure target about 850 to 1010); federal forest service pages on El Yunque, its coquí frogs, parrots and hurricanes (public domain); an original problem and solution article on protecting the forest; the base text's own CC BY texts on rainforest structure, adapted; two original Curio Class rainforest adventure stories in one genre (2,000 to 4,000 words each, measure target about 850 to 1010), one told in the first person by a girl lost for a day in El Yunque, one in the third person about a boy following a parrot survey, on the same theme of learning to read the forest. How plants, animals and people in the forest interact, from specific information (RI.5.3); the scientist's chronology compared with the problem and solution article (RI.5.5); answers found quickly across several print and digital sources, teacher-chosen first, then a supervised search (RI.5.7). How each narrator's point of view shapes how the same kind of event is described (RL.5.6); the two adventure stories compared on how they approach the theme (RL.5.9); a literary essay on how one story's sensory language builds its setting, quoting the text (W.5.2 and W.5.9 used again). Research on one way to protect the forest from several sources, summarized or paraphrased in notes, with a list of sources (W.5.7, W.5.8). Writing: a first-person narrative of a day in the canopy as a researcher or animal of the forest, with dialogue, pacing, transitional clauses and sensory detail (W.5.3 used again); planned, critiqued, rewritten and edited, typed at least two pages in a sitting, and partners exchange drafts and respond through a shared document on a teacher-managed route with no student account before publishing on a teacher-managed class page (W.5.6, continued from unit 1). Varieties of English named in the stories' dialogue and narration (L.5.3b; compared and contrasted in unit 4). Review: e5-several-main-ideas, e5-academic-words (biodiversity, canopy, species), e5-quote-accurately, e5-conventions in editing. The unit's word list
3 | Athletes who led change. "What made these athletes leaders of change, beyond winning?" | 40 | e5-multiple-accounts; e5-reasons-to-points; e5-integrate-several; e5-opinion; e5-presenting | RI.5.6; RI.5.8; SL.5.3; RI.5.9; W.5.1; SL.5.4; SL.5.5; SL.5.6 | Not used (ELA). Leans on social studies grade 5 (civil rights; sourcing and corroboration)
Anchors: an original Curio Class biography of Jackie Robinson read whole (about 15,000 to 20,000 words, measure target about 850 to 1010); his 1949 testimony to a House committee (public domain), short newspaper reports of 1947 and a teammate's later memory, as several accounts (each report and the memory cleared by a copyright renewal search; any that is not clear is replaced by Curio Class's own account built from the facts it reports); short original biographies of other athletes who led change (Roberto Clemente, Wilma Rudolph, Jim Abbott); a short Curio Class explainer on segregation in the 1940s, since social studies teaches it later in the year. Several accounts of Robinson's first season analysed for the points of view they represent (RI.5.6); which reasons and evidence support which point in texts arguing what made an athlete a leader, on the argument map, introduced here (RI.5.8), and a speaker's points summarized with how each claim is supported (SL.5.3); several biographies joined to speak and write knowledgeably, tracking which idea came from which source (RI.5.9). Writing: an opinion essay on which qualities most made the athlete a leader of change, planned on the argument map, reasons in logical order, linked with clauses (consequently, specifically), and concluded (W.5.1a to d); an informative essay comparing how two athletes led change, from several biographies (W.5.2 used again, RI.5.9). Talk: a multimedia presentation on another athlete who led change, opinion and reasons sequenced logically, with a visual display, and speech adapted from the small group to the class audience in formal English (SL.5.4, SL.5.5, SL.5.6). Review: e5-quote-accurately, e5-research, e5-writing-process, e5-grammar
4 | Water we share. "How much of Earth's water can people use, and how should communities share it?" | 33 | e5-visual-multimedia | RL.5.7 | Not used (ELA). Leans on science grade 5 (Earth's water; protecting resources)
Anchors: two original Curio Class survival stories in one genre, both in a drought, one a girl's family keeping its farm through a dry year and one a boy's ranching family on a shrinking river, 1,500 to 4,000 words each (measure target about 850 to 1010), with speakers of different varieties of English; an original illustrated story of a river told by the people who live along it, with its illustrations doing part of the telling; poems about rivers and rain, including Langston Hughes's "The Negro Speaks of Rivers" (1921, public domain) and original poems in the voices of people with too little water; federal water-agency and geological survey pages on Earth's water budget and on the Colorado River, shared by seven US states and Mexico (public domain); several short accounts of one drought year on the river (a farmer, a city water manager, a tribal water office, a federal report). What the illustrations and a short read-aloud video add to the river story's meaning, tone and beauty (RL.5.7). The two drought stories compared on how they approach the same theme and how each narrator's point of view shapes the telling (review of e5-genre-compare and e5-narrator-influence), and the varieties of English in their dialogue compared and contrasted, what each tells about the speaker and the setting, with respect for each (L.5.3b, continued from unit 2). Earth's water shown as hundredths on a grid (97 of 100 parts salt water; most of the rest frozen), and a short federal talk on the river summarized from its words and its display (review of e5-several-main-ideas, SL.5.2). Research on where the class's own community gets its water, from several sources with a list of sources (review of e5-research, e5-locate-sources); an opinion essay on the most important way the community can protect that water, planned on the argument map (review of e5-opinion, e5-reasons-to-points); the several drought accounts analysed (review of e5-multiple-accounts, e5-integrate-several). The year's spoken and written products: a short announcement on protecting the community's water, recorded as audio on a classroom device (review of e5-presenting), and a leaflet presented live. Review: e5-figurative and e5-whole-structure in the poems, e5-conventions in the announcement script. The unit's word list

science-grade-5
# | Unit and compelling question | Days | Skills | Owned standards | C3
What students learn
1 | Matter too small to see. "What is everything made of, if we cannot see the pieces, and does any of it vanish when it changes?" Anchor: a flat ball weighed, pumped up with air, and weighed again: it is heavier, though all we added was air we cannot see | 17 | s5-particles; s5-conserve-weight | 5-PS1-1; 5-PS1-2 | Practices: developing and using models (particles); using mathematics and computational thinking (measuring and graphing weights). Crosscutting: scale, proportion and quantity; cause and effect
A model that matter is made of particles too small to see, built from evidence the class gathers: air pumped into a ball makes it firmer and heavier; air in a closed syringe can be squeezed; sugar dissolved in water vanishes from sight but the water tastes sweet (the teacher's food-safe demonstration, or described) and salt water left to dry leaves salt behind. Dots drawn for the parts too small to see, closer or farther apart, revised as each piece of evidence arrives; the idea of very small scale. Then weights measured and graphed before and after heating, cooling and mixing: ice melting in a sealed bag, water frozen in a sealed bag (the cooling case), salt dissolving in water, and baking soda and vinegar in a bottle with a balloon on top, in small amounts (a quarter teaspoon of baking soda, a tablespoon of vinegar) so the balloon barely fills and the scale reads the same to the gram; the same mix in an open bottle, which does lose weight, is the comparison; the total stays the same. Students compare the complete system before and after and investigate any difference (escaping matter, a spill, the scale's reading, a change in the apparatus) rather than assume every lower reading means a gas escaped; the particle model explains the open bottle
2 | Where a tree comes from. "Where does a growing tree's matter come from, and where does it go when the tree dies?" Anchor: the tree-in-a-pot experiment Jan Baptist van Helmont ran in Brussels in the 1600s: a willow grew from 5 pounds to about 169 in five years while its soil lost less than a pound | 24 | s5-plant-matter; s5-matter-movement; s5-food-energy-sun | 5-LS1-1; 5-LS2-1; 5-PS3-1 | Practices: engaging in argument from evidence (plant matter); developing and using models (food web and matter cycle; energy from the sun). Crosscutting: energy and matter; systems and system models
An argument that plants get the materials to grow chiefly from air and water, not soil: the historical data (evidence against soil supplying most of the added matter; the experimenter concluded it came from water alone, a reading later evidence corrected), plants grown in water alone, and the class's own pots, set up in week 1 with dry-weighed soil, weighed again at the unit's end with the soil dried (compare plant gain and dry-soil change within measurement limits, with no promised result); then the air half: a supplied, sourced comparison of plants grown with the usual air and with more of the gas plants take in from air (as greenhouse growers add it), the plants with more of it growing heavier; students join the soil, water and air evidence and the particle model's air-is-matter in one argument; roots and leaves are the structures that take in the water and air; light is the energy, not the material. A model of matter moving among plants, animals, decomposers and the environment: the food web (who eats whom, with decomposers) and the matter cycle, including matter entering and leaving living things through air, water, food and waste, with a sealed jar ecosystem or compost bottle as first-hand evidence over weeks; students use the model to explain how several kinds of living things in one place each meet their needs, then revise an explanation for a supplied case of a new consumer with no predator (rabbits brought to Australia). Then models (diagrams, flow charts) showing that the energy in animals' food, used for growth, repair, motion and keeping warm, was once the sun's: sun to grass to cow to person, each arrow an energy transfer; energy flows through while matter cycles. Nature of science: one careful measurement overturned an old belief
3 | Mystery materials. "How can we tell substances apart, and how do we know when mixing makes a new one?" Anchor: four unlabelled jars of white powder in a kitchen (baking soda, cornstarch, sugar, salt), one of which must not go into the cake | 17 | s5-identify-materials; s5-new-substances | 5-PS1-3; 5-PS1-4 | Practices: planning and carrying out investigations (both rows). Crosscutting: scale, proportion and quantity through standard units (identification); cause and effect (mixing); patterns supports comparisons
Observations and measurements to identify materials by their properties: the four powders, and metals, minerals and liquids, tested for color, hardness, reflectivity (shine), whether they conduct electricity (a battery-and-bulb tester) or heat, whether a magnet pulls them, and whether they dissolve; a property table whose pattern names an unknown sample. Then an investigation, planned by the class, to decide whether mixing makes a new substance: before testing, students record the starting materials' properties, the comparison being made, what stays the same, what will be measured and that each comparison runs three trials; afterwards they record the resulting properties and use the controlled comparisons to justify their claim (opened by a retrieval task on the fair-test routine of grades 3 and 4): bubbles of a new gas, a color change, a solid forming or warmth (baking soda and vinegar, milk and lemon juice, steel wool and water rusting) show a new substance; a mixture that can be separated again (sand and salt, iron filings and sand) does not; the particle model explains both
4 | Earth and sky. "What do falling objects, shadows and stars tell us about Earth's shape and motion?" Anchor: the class's shadow stick (the grade 1 shadow stick returns on purpose, now measured): through each day the shadow shrinks, swings round and grows again, and the shadow measured at the same sun time each month since the first month of school has changed; and an Australia fall diagram labeled "invented for practice" showing local "down" too | 20 | s5-gravity-down; s5-shadows-sky; s5-star-brightness | 5-PS2-1; 5-ESS1-2; 5-ESS1-1 | Practices: engaging in argument from evidence (gravity; star brightness); analyzing and interpreting data (shadows, day length, stars). Crosscutting: cause and effect; patterns; scale, proportion and quantity
An argument that Earth's gravity pulls objects down, where down means toward the center of the round Earth: plumb lines and dropped objects everywhere fall toward the ground, and on a globe that is toward the middle; force arrows now point to Earth's center. Data in graphs revealing daily and seasonal patterns: shadow length and direction each hour, day length through the year, and stars or constellations seen only in some months; the Earth-sun-moon model as Earth spinning (day and night, moving shadows) and moving around the sun (different night-sky stars by season), checked with a globe and lamp. Then an argument that the sun looks far brighter than other stars because it is much closer: two equal flashlights at different distances in a dark hall; distances given as the time light takes to arrive (about 8 minutes from the sun, more than 4 years from the nearest other star, many more years from the bright stars of the night sky) and a scale model (if the sun were a grain of sand on the classroom table, the nearest star would be a grain of sand in a town far away), with no number over 1,000,000 and powers of ten only after math teaches them; light from a star travels to the eye and less of it arrives from farther away; the sun is a star
5 | Earth's systems and its water. "How do water, air, land and life shape each other, and how much of Earth's water can we use?" Anchor: from space Earth looks blue, yet towns run short of water to drink; and one mountain range (the Cascades of Washington) has rain forest on its west side and dry land on its east | 15 | s5-sphere-interactions; s5-water-distribution | 5-ESS2-1; 5-ESS2-2 | Practices: developing and using models (Earth's systems); using mathematics and computational thinking (graphing amounts of water). Crosscutting: systems and system models; scale, proportion and quantity
A model, through one worked example, of how two of Earth's systems interact: the named example is the Cascades, where mountains force wet ocean air up so clouds and rain form on the west side (land and air, with water); further cases met briefly (the ocean shapes coastlines and local climate and supports life; rain and wind wear down land). Rock and land, water, air and living things are each a system; two at a time. Then how much of Earth's water is salt water and how much fresh, and where the fresh water is, from a supplied table of shares already given as parts out of 100: two hundred-square grids and two bar graphs students make, all of Earth's water (about 97 salt water, nearly all ocean, and 3 fresh), then fresh water as its own 100 parts (about 69 ice caps/glaciers/permanent snow, 30 fresh groundwater, 1 surface and other fresh water, including lakes, rivers and swamps but also other stores). The sourced small-reservoir inset separates lakes (0.26) and rivers (0.006 parts per 100 fresh), using supplied same-scale axes and decimal shares, not percentage calculations. Locate polar ice within the combined ice category and name each boundary reservoir; source: USGS water-distribution table after Shiklomanov (1993). No air-water quantities are assessed
6 | Protecting water by design. "How can a community keep its water clean, and how do we know our filter works?" Anchor: after a storm, brown runoff pours from a parking lot into a stream that a town downstream drinks from | 18 | s5-protect-resources; s5-design-test | 5-ESS3-1; 3-5-ETS1-3 | Practices: obtaining, evaluating and communicating information (protecting resources); planning and carrying out investigations (fair tests of a prototype). Crosscutting: systems and system models
Information combined about how communities use science ideas to protect Earth's resources and environment: protecting watersheds, cleaning up runoff, restoring wetlands, cutting air pollution, saving water; each action tied to the Earth system it protects; the fresh water case from unit 5. Then the band's full design cycle from that problem: a filter that cleans muddy water (the named pick; a rain catcher that slows runoff is the second choice), with solutions generated and compared, then fair tests of the prototype planned and run with one variable changed and the rest held the same, several trials each, data graphed (a line plot of repeated measurements) before a change is made, and the failure points that show what to improve. Nature of science: repeated trials, because one result can mislead

social-studies-grade-5
# | Unit and compelling question | Days | Skills | Owned standards | C3
What students learn
1 | The colonies and their neighbors. "Whose land was it, and who decided?" | 14 | ss5-colonial-encounters; ss5-colonial-regions | MA.5.T1.1; MA.5.T1.2; MA.5.T1.3; MA.5.T1.4 | Practiced: D2.His.1, D2.Geo (maps), D1, D3.4, D4.1
English settlers and Native nations (the Wampanoag and the 1621 harvest were met in kindergarten and grade 3; this unit adds the treaty and land-loss record from 1621 to King Philip's War): disease, two views of land, trade, the Pequot War and King Philip's War from English and Native sources; why different colonies were founded, one founder researched (Penn, Williams or Winthrop); why English language and political ideas most shaped the later country; the 13 colonies on a map; how climate and land shaped crops and work in the New England, Middle and Southern colonies; family, hired, indentured and enslaved labor, on a map with a key for crops and labor
2 | Slavery in the colonies. "How did enslaved and free Africans shape the colonies, and how did they seek freedom?" | 12 | ss5-slavery-colonies | MA.5.T1.5; MA.5.T1.6; MA.5.T1.7; MA.5.T1.7a; MA.5.T1.7b; MA.5.T1.7c; MA.5.T1.7d | Practiced: D2.His.1, D1, D3.4, D4.1
How slavery began in the English colonies: Africans forced across the Atlantic, the first recorded in Virginia in 1619 (not the start of slavery in the Americas), and colony laws from the 1660s that made slavery lifelong and passed it from mother to child, so that it became tied to race (5.T1.5); slavery legal in all thirteen colonies in the late colonial period (Georgia from 1751), and ownership widespread but uneven, many early leaders among them; the Triangular Trade (retrieved from grade 3) and the Middle Passage; enslaved and free people compared directly: held as property, sold away from families, denied schooling, punished for reading or escape, and the revolts these led to (5.T1.7a); skilled artisans hired out with owners keeping the pay (7b); freedom after service, by grant or by suing, Elizabeth Freeman, Quock Walker, Prince Hall's petitions (7c); gradual emancipation in the North and Massachusetts's courts ending slavery by 1783, free Black people's families, property and wages (7d)
3 | The road to revolution. "When is breaking a law the right thing to do?" | 17 | ss5-sourcing-corroboration; ss5-revolution-causes | D2.His.10.3-5; D2.His.11.3-5; D2.His.16.3-5; MA.5.T2.1; MA.5.T2.1a; MA.5.T2.1b; MA.5.T2.1c; MA.5.T2.1d; MA.5.T2.2; MA.5.T2.3 | Owned: D2.His.10, 11, 16. Practiced: D1, D3.4, D4.1
Source each document unprompted (who, when, for whom, why), then weigh two accounts of one event, a Boston engraving of the 1770 King Street shooting and a trial witness's testimony, and write a claim with evidence from both. The French and Indian War, each side with Native allies; its debts, the taxes and the Proclamation of 1763; British policies and colonial responses on the timeline; taxation and representation; the Stamp Act Congress, the Sons of Liberty, the Tea Party, the Suffolk Resolves, the fighting at Lexington, Concord and Bunker Hill, and the British leaving Boston in March 1776 (all of 5.T2.1c here); women's roles in full (5.T2.1d); colonial legislatures, town meetings and charters; a 1770s map of Boston with its people (Samuel Adams, Crispus Attucks, Paul Revere, Phillis Wheatley), and from sources how two of them shaped an event located on the map (5.T2.2)
4 | Independence and a first government. "Did the Revolution keep the Declaration's promise?" | 16 | ss5-independence-war | MA.5.T2.4; MA.5.T2.5; MA.5.T2.5a; MA.5.T2.5b; MA.5.T2.5c; MA.5.T2.6; MA.5.T2.7; MA.5.T2.8; MA.5.T2.9; D2.Eco.11.3-5 | Owned: D2.Eco.11. Practiced: D2.His.10, 11 (unit 3), D1, D3.4, D4.1
The Declaration's argument and its ideas of equality, rights and the rule of law, and who those words left out in 1776 (retrieved from grade 3's founding ideas); Trenton, Saratoga, Valley Forge and Yorktown on a map with what each changed; why the Americans won; Loyalists and neutrals, Native and African people on both sides; leaders compared (Adams, Franklin, George III, Jefferson, Hamilton, Madison, Washington, Lafayette); the Articles of Confederation: why the states adopted them in 1781 and what that league of states first offered, then their weaknesses and why they failed; inflation, then deflation and unemployment after the war, and Shays' Rebellion as a push toward the Convention; personal finance touch, no added day: what rising prices did to a family's savings and debts
5 | The Constitution. "How did the Constitution try to keep power from growing too strong, and whose rights did it protect?" | 16 | ss5-constitution | MA.5.T3.1; MA.5.T3.2; MA.5.T3.3; MA.5.T3.4; MA.5.T3.5; MA.5.T3.6; D2.Civ.3.3-5; D2.Civ.1.3-5 | Owned: D2.Civ.1, 3. Practiced: D2.His.10, D1, D3.4, D4.1
The Preamble and parts of the Constitution, and the principles they hold; three branches, what each does and how each checks the others; what federal, state and local governments each do; the Convention's debates over power, state size, the Senate and slavery, the Three-Fifths Compromise keeping the states together while strengthening slavery; voting and property rights not given to women; how an amendment is added (proposed by Congress, ratified by three-fourths of the states), with the Bill of Rights as the first case; the Bill of Rights read and the freedoms it guarantees explained, then a claim with evidence about why one amendment was added in 1791
6 | The new republic grows. "What made the young United States stronger, and at whose cost?" | 17 | ss5-first-presidents; ss5-republic-growth; ss5-war-of-1812; ss5-new-england-industry | MA.5.T4.1; MA.5.T4.2; MA.5.T4.3; MA.5.T4.3a; MA.5.T4.3b; MA.5.T4.4; D2.Eco.6.3-5; D2.Eco.8.3-5; D2.Eco.13.3-5; D2.Geo.11.3-5 | Owned: D2.Eco.6, 8, 13; D2.Geo.11. Practiced: D2.His.10, D1, D3.4, D4.1
Washington, Adams and Jefferson: the first cabinet, the first parties, the peaceful handover of 1801, the Louisiana Purchase, judged by what each set out to do; that Washington and Jefferson enslaved people and Adams did not. The Purchase and Lewis and Clark traced by latitude and longitude and compass directions from the journals, with Sacagawea and York, and the nations who knew and governed that land. The War of 1812's causes and events and the national pride it built, Tecumseh's alliance with Britain noted for unit 7. New England's mills, machines, whaling, shipping and China trade on a map; productivity and pay; benefits and costs, including costs to people outside the deal; Northern mills spinning cotton grown by enslaved people
7 | Removal, the spread of slavery, and the fight against it. "How did people resist injustice in the early 1800s?" | 16 | ss5-native-sovereignty-removal; ss5-slavery-expansion; ss5-abolition | MA.5.T4.5; MA.5.T4.5a; MA.5.T4.5b; MA.5.T4.5c; MA.5.T4.5d; D2.His.4.3-5; D2.His.5.3-5; MA.5.T5.1; MA.5.T5.3; D2.His.6.3-5; D2.Civ.14.3-5 | Owned: D2.His.4, 5, 6; D2.Civ.14. Practiced: D2.His.10, 11, D1, D3.4, D4.1
Tecumseh's call (1810); Jackson and the Indian Removal Act of 1830 (removal was met in grades 2 and 3 as a forced move and the Cherokee Nation's homelands; this unit adds the law: the Act, Worcester v. Georgia (1832) and the failure to enforce it); the Trail of Tears (1838) and the removal of the Muscogee, Choctaw, Chickasaw and Seminole nations; the Mashpee Revolt of 1833; why people of the time differed, from their context. Northern states ending slavery while it spread west and south with cotton; the 1808 ban and the trade inside the country that sold about a million people; free and slave states on a map through the 1850s. Tubman, Douglass, Sojourner Truth and others, Black and white, and their means (escape, speeches, newspapers, petitions, courts); Douglass's own words and how his purpose shaped them; those means set beside ones people use today
